To access the fourth element of a linked list in Java, you need to traverse the list starting from the head node. Since linked lists do not support random access like arrays, you must iterate through the nodes one by one until you reach the desired position.

Here is a simple example using a custom `Node` class and a `LinkedList` class:

```java
class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

public class LinkedList {
    private Node head;

    // Method to get the nth element (1-based index)
    public int getNthElement(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Index must be positive");
        }

        Node current = head;
        int count = 1;

        while (current != null) {
            if (count == n) {
                return current.data;
            }
            current = current.next;
            count++;
        }

        throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("Index out of bounds");
    }

    // Helper method to add elements for demonstration
    public void add(int data) {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
        } else {
            Node current = head;
            while (current.next != null) {
                current = current.next;
            }
            current.next = newNode;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList list = new LinkedList();
        list.add(10);
        list.add(20);
        list.add(30);
        list.add(40);
        list.add(50);

        try {
            int fourthElement = list.getNthElement(4);
            System.out.println("The fourth element is: " + fourthElement);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

**Key Points:**
1.  **Traversal**: You start at the `head` and follow the `next` pointers.
2.  **Loop**: A `while` loop continues until the current node is `null` (end of list)
